<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heliand</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 830</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Heliand is an Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> epic poem of about 6,000 lines, written around 830, that retells the life of Christ from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> in Germanic alliterative verse. It presents Christ as a lord and his disciples as loyal retainers, adapting the story to the values of the recently converted Saxons. It is the largest surviving work of Old Saxon literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heliand.jpg" alt="Heliand" width="276" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: German Historical Museum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heliand_Excerpt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The poem was probably written at the request of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, as an answer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> ambivalence toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The Saxons had been forced to convert in the late 8th and early 9th centuries, after 33 years of conflict between the Saxons under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. Around the time of writing the Saxon stelinga, the lower social <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, rose in revolt. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> praefatio, first printed by Matthias Flacius in 1562 and generally deemed authentic, says the emperor commanded a Saxon poet to render the Old and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testaments</a> in verse in his own tongue.</p><p>The poet worked not from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> directly but from the pseudo-Tatian Gospel harmony, and knew the commentaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrabanus-maurus">Rabanus Maurus</a>. Scholars disagree whether he was unlearned, like Caedmon, or a Christian Saxon able to read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> who followed the calling of a scop, or minstrel. Early scholars such as Wilhelm Braune held that he also wrote the Old Saxon Genesis, but consensus has moved away from that view.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The work is divided into fitts, or songs. Repeated words and phrases, and sentences that begin in the middle of a line for the sake of alliteration, read awkwardly on the page but suit a poem sung after dinner in a mead hall or monastery. Neumes written above the text in one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> suggest that it may have been sung. Scholars disagree whether its tone shows a Germanized Christianity, with its traditions of fighting and enmity, or a Christianized Germany, given its message that it is better to be meek than mighty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Two nearly complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> survive. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a> manuscript in the British Library, probably written in the second half of the 10th century, breaks off in the journey to Emmaus; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> manuscript, formerly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> and now in the Bavarian State Library, begins at line 85. Fragments came to light at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1881, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a> in 1894, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a> in 1979 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 2006.</p><p>Franciscus Junius transcribed a fragment in 1587, George Hickes first printed the poem in 1705, and Johann Andreas Schmeller published the first modern edition in 1830, giving the work its name. Eduard Sievers's edition of 1877, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> the two main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> side by side, became the standard. Many historians agree that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> owned a copy and cited it to encourage translating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> into the vernacular.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heliand” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Old Saxon epic <b>Heliand</b>, written around 830, retold the life of Christ in Saxon alliterative verse, presenting Christ as a lord and the disciples as his retainers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Heliand</b> and Passion plays followed in the Middle Ages, and the Passion has been a theme in film since La Passion (France, 1903).</blockquote><a href="/work/new-testament">New Testament · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
